Whenever the county engineer or his/her representative determines that activity on a property does not comply with the approved development and construction plans, he/she may issue a notice of violation and/or a stop work order. Whenever the county engineer determines that the drainage system has been unlawfully altered, causing inadequate drainage, he/she may issue a notice of violation and/or a stop work order. A stop work order or notice of violation of the provisions of this article or of any rule or regulation adopted pursuant thereto, shall be directed to the owner of the property or his/her agent and shall:
(1) Be in writing;
(2) Include a description of the property sufficient to identify where the violation has occurred;
(3) State that, if repairs, construction or alterations are not completed within a reasonable time period specified by the inspector, summons shall be issued for the owner to appear in court. Each day the violation continues shall constitute a new and separate violation. However, in the judgment of the county engineer, where the violation is willful, in wanton disregard of the provisions of this article or constitutes a public health and safety hazard, he/she may issue a court summons in lieu of a notice of violation.
(Ord. No. 97-1, § 45, 1-28-1997)
